    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my realtor just told me i need to set up an escrow account for the house i'm buying in toronto. whats that actually is and why cant i just pay the seller directly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "An escrow account, in this case, is a trust account that holds funds until certain conditions are met, like the sale being finalized and the property being transferred. It's a common practice in real estate, especially in Toronto, where the market can be quite volatile. You see, escrow accounts are like a protective shield, safeguarding the funds until the deal is sealed, just like a wise old wizard guarding a treasure trove of gold coins.\n\nNow, as for why you can't just pay the seller directly, it's because escrow accounts help prevent disputes and ensure a smooth transaction. Think of it like a game of hide-and-seek between the buyer and seller. The escrow account is like a neutral third party, holding the funds until both sides agree on the terms. If the seller tries to pull a fast one, the escrow account can be like a pesky little goblin, holding onto the funds until the deal is fair and square.\n\nIn Toronto, the law requires escrow accounts to be set up for certain transactions, like buying a house. It's a safeguard to protect both the buyer and seller from any potential issues. So, while it might seem like an extra step, it's actually a necessary process",
